  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nSunday is slotted to be a down day for shower activity with a \nbrief period of shortwave ridging and some slight dry air \nadvection from the northwest. This will lead to considerably less \nclouds and virtually no rain chances. Models continue to forecast \none after another northern Pacific waves being ejected \nsoutheastward toward the U.S. West Coast with the next potential \nsystem reaching the California coast early Monday before reaching \nour region Monday night into Tuesday. This may even be followed by\nanother system during the second half of next week. However, it \nis too early to have any good sense on the track of these systems \nas they could move directly through the Desert Southwest or mostly\nmiss us to the north, so continued monitoring will be needed. \nNBM/WPC PoPs of 30-70% are still in the forecast for the Tuesday \ndisturbance, but that could easily change depending on the \neventual track. So far, any system that may move through is not \nexpected to carry anything more than average moisture, so any \nrainfall amounts are likely to be on the lighter side with no\nflooding concerns expected at this time.  \n\nWith the aforementioned shortwave ridging briefly moving in \nSunday, temperatures will slightly warm into the middle to upper \n70s through Monday. A more significant cooling trend is then \nexpected by the middle of the week as the series of weather \nsystems moving in from the northwest will likely usher in a \ncooler air mass with afternoon highs trending more towards the \nupper 60s to low 70s.",
